  "result": "I've written the 30-minute lesson on O3, a five-item quiz with its key, and a cheat sheet. I didn't open the lesson in a browser or open any of its links, and I haven't measured its length. By my estimate it runs about 3,500 words, which would fit the 30-minute bench range (2,700 to 6,750).\n\n**Lesson: `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`.** By the end, you can decide where each piece of the makerspace assistant's information lives: in the context, in a saved note, or in a searchable library. It covers:\n- **Context window:** what the model sees on a call, and why you pay for it.\n- **Between steps:** the program re-sends the whole history on every call, with a worked count of input tokens.\n- **Full windows:** trimming, compaction and lean tool results.\n- **Between sessions:** notes as memory.\n- **Retrieval:** searching the manuals for the right chunk.\n- **Practice:** a worked example for a 3D printer request, then one for you to do on a vinyl cutter request.\n\nIt also has four figures and a check question in each section.\n\n**Quiz 0002** is ready when you want it, and the key is stored with it. Ask me for it in the chat.\n\n**Sources:** Anthropic's context-engineering post is the primary source. I got its content from search summaries and didn't open the full text. The \"Lost in the Middle\" paper and a context-window explainer are also cited, and all three are in `RESOURCES.md`. No community link was found, so the lesson points you to the makerspace staff.\n\n**Gap:** I haven't read any provider's pricing or prompt-caching documentation, so that's the next thing to look up for your budget numbers.\n\nAsk me anything about the lesson. Do you want a follow-up lesson on writing tool descriptions for `search_manuals` and `save_note`?",
